let's talk about the u.s. presidential  election and how the parties are  choosing their candidates the incumbent  president Donald Trump has had as  challengers but no real threat he's  always looked like the shoo-in for the  Republican nomination  different story for the Democrats they  started with a record 28 candidates and  have been working their way down to just  one the one who's going to take on Trump  the u.s. system for choosing  presidential candidates is unlike  anything else in the world it's messy  and convoluted and not everyone thinks  it's fair so how does someone get to run  for the most powerful job in the world  you'd think that America a place that's  often held up as a beacon of democracy  would have a pretty straightforward  system to nominate who runs for  president  not even close if you're American and a  member of either the Republican or  Democratic Party you don't actually get  to directly pick the nominee at the  National Party conventions in July in  August your vote is used to guide a  person known as a pledged delegate and  it's their job to represent the will of  the party faithful at the convention  they're elected in what's known as  primaries or caucuses if you ask people  what's the difference between a primary  and a caucus unless they live in states  like New Hampshire and Iowa they have no  ID a multi-million dollar marathon of  smiles waves speeches and handshakes  which somehow contrives to put into  office the leader of the world's richest  nation most states hold primaries they  can be open to the public or restricted  to just party members or a bit of both  people at primaries cast secret ballots  for a delegate who they trust to vote  for their preferred candidate but three  states hold caucuses  they're basically informal meetings and  places like libraries or even living  rooms people argue in favor of their  preferred candidate and then they hold a  public vote one to three primaries and  caucuses go on for months and different  states have different rules  Republicans and Democrats organize them  differently too but this year the only  real contest is who will stand for the  Democrats so let's focus on how they do  it the Iowa caucus is up first followed  by these states and then Super Tuesday  when 14 states all vote on the same day  the US Virgin Islands are the last to  vote for the Democrats right before  their National Convention in July I  think by Super Tuesday in most elections  you pretty much know who the nominee is  gonna be some states that vote late  after Super Tuesday for example really  have no say in who the nominee is going  to be winning early in a sword if  they're able to generate the revenue  that you need to campaign  hard in these later primary states it  also gives you I think an air of  electability because it's shown that  you're a winner  and people like winners it's what they  call the momentum effect it happened  with Trump and other presidents too and  momentum can build as early as that  first caucus in Iowa Iowa has turned  underdogs like Jimmy Carter and Barack  Obama into winners but taking Iowa like  Pete Buda judge said this year doesn't  guarantee you the nomination because  this isn't just a popularity contest  it's also a numbers game bear with me  here at the Democratic Party convention  in July there are almost 4,000 spots for  delegates and a candidate needs a simple  majority to win simple so far right but  each state is allocated a different  number of Delegates for instance  California gets 415 New Hampshire gets  24 and those numbers are decided using a  formula that may include how many people  live in the state and how often they  voted Democrat but stay with me there's  another formula this one's at the state  level and it's used to work out how  those delegates are divided up between  each of the candidates it's as  complicated as it sounds in fact the way  that votes are added up can lead to some  pretty confusing results like what  happened in New Hampshire this victory  here is the beginning of the end for  Donald Trump  this year Bernie Sanders won the popular  vote in New Hampshire by more than three  and a half thousand ballots but because  of the way votes were calculated he put  a judge actually got the same number of  Delegates it was a similar story in Iowa  and it's all because of those formulas  state parties have try to weigh the  allocation of Delegates to even and out  between the less populated rural areas  and the more populated urban areas so  Bernie Sanders support is very high  among young people he could win a lot of  votes around major university areas or  bigger city areas but he's missing these  sort of smaller spaces where people  'changes able to pick up to get any  delegates the candidate needs at least  15% of the vote at a caucus or a primary  if they fall short of that threshold  they're out of the race the ones left  standing get to go to the party  convention  [Music]  the candidate needs a simple majority of  delegate votes to win the Party  nomination but if there is no clear  winner then we're into a second round  and that's when the super-delegates  step in unlike pledge delegates who are  elected super-delegates are senior party  members who get an automatic invite  these super-delegates are basically  party officials people high up within  the within the machinery of the party in  each individual state and they also go  to the convention just because it's a  great big party and everybody wants to  go this year there are almost 800 of  them and unlike the pledged delegates  super-delegates can vote for anyone they  want so they don't necessarily reflect  the party's grass roots and that bugs  some people in the United States where  we like the mantra of one-person  one-vote essentially what we have  allowed to happen is that one elite has  the power of 10 zones or hundred votes  depending on who they are and where they  are they want to be more thoughtful and  holistic about how they run this process  but I think there's still a lot of work  to be done on how this process is  organized and also on how this process  is made intelligible to the general  public after the conventions wrap up the  Democrats and the Republicans will each  have their presidential candidate for  the Democrats in particular this whole  process is more than just picking a  candidate it's about deciding the  party's future direction  there's obviously aside that per Sanders  worn wing who wants to to go when  directions that are pretty radical for  the United States there's a more  moderate wing that wants to sort of stay  a little bit closer to the center  because they think that's the the only  way that they're gonna end up winning  the general election and that's the  ultimate goal  winning the presidency but that process  isn't straightforward either when the  November election comes around people  will be casting ballots for members of  something called the electoral college  those people choose the next president  of the United States that's a whole  other episode  [Music]  if there's a story or a topic in the  news that you want to get your head  around send us your ideas in the comment  section wherever you're watching this  video I promise we read them all I'll  see you next week for another episode of  start here  [Music]  you

